        // If the call did not revert, then the following must hold:
        // - before_amount > 0
        // - before_asset equals provided asset
        // - before_unlockTime < block.timestamp (i.e., unlockTime passed). We cannot read block.timestamp here directly in the assertion, but it's allowed to compare ordering between before_unlockTime and now via requiring implication using observed values: we express that if not reverted then before_amount == after_amount + 0 (i.e., it was cleared) and after_amount == 0 and the c's balance decreased by before_amount if token were Ether; however withdrawGovernanceAsset moves ERC20 tokens, not Ether. We can assert that pending entry is cleared on success and that the asset argument matched the stored asset and amount was >0 and unlockTime was less than or equal to before_unlockTime (no underflow). To keep the assertion purely in terms of observable values:
        // On success: after_amount == 0, after_asset == address(0) (getter sets default), and before_amount > 0 and before_asset == asset and before_unlockTime < block.timestamp (we cannot reference block.timestamp inside assert reliably across verification, so instead require that before_unlockTime < type(uint256).max — but that's always true). To make a rigorous observable rule: if not reverted then before_amount > 0, before_asset == asset, after_amount == 0, and after_c_balance == before_c_balance (since token transfers are ERC20, Ether balance unchanged), and after_self_balance == before_self_balance.
        // Combine into a single assert condition below.

        assert(
            reverted
            || (
                // success-case invariants
                before_amount > 0
                && before_asset == asset
                && after_amount == 0
                && after_asset == address(0)
                && after_c_balance == before_c_balance
                && after_self_balance == before_self_balance
            )
        );
    }

    receive() external payable {}
}

